NAME
    mkdir - make a directory

SYNOPSIS
    mkdir path
    mkdir -s sectors path

DESCRIPTION
    Makes a new, empty directory: 16 sectors, room for 254 entries
    besides "." and "..". Quiet on success; "cannot mkdir" when the
    name exists, the parent is missing or full, or the name is not
    valid.

    -s makes it that many sectors, 1 to 64: 16 entries a sector, less
    "." and ".." (-s 4: 62, -s 64: 1022). A directory's size is fixed
    when it is made, since files lie right behind it; for a bigger one
    later, make a new directory with -s and mv the files into it. The
    root stays 4 sectors, 62 entries.

    mkdir is /bin/mkdir (2026-11-03). Without -s it asks the OS (the
    MKDIR syscall, 8, which cp -r uses too); with -s it writes the
    directory on the card itself, as pack does: the new extent at the
    free pointer, the free pointer raised, then the entry in the
    parent. A failure ends with status 1 (a running script stops).

    As a /bin program it runs in the program area at $5000, as dir,
    cat and del do: a program loaded there (load) is overwritten. The
    shell also has a built-in mkdir path (no -s), used when there is no
    /bin/mkdir: a fresh card needs it to make /bin before any /bin
    program exists.

LIMITS
    Names are 1..12 characters; the path at most 62. Refused while a
    file is open for writing, which includes a > or >> redirect and a
    pipe: a directory is allocated at the same free pointer (see
    shell). With -s: "mkdir: -s not with > or a pipe".
    The OS's own mkdir (the built-in, the syscall, cp -r) always makes
    16 sectors.

EXAMPLES
    mkdir /t
    mkdir SUB           in the current directory
    mkdir -s 64 /work   room for 1022 entries

NOTES
    Changed from the P8X version: nothing is printed on success (the
    P8X said "DIR CREATED").
    The command is the Y1/OS shell's own (os/y1os.asm; os/y1os.c); the page is
    ported from P8X os/man/mkdir 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    rmdir, cd, dir, cp, shell
